 1 big thing: Trump promises cheaper beef 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios President Trump said today that he would temporarily increase tariff-free beef imports and that he had secured a commitment to sell the meat at 25% below current market prices, Axios' Nathan Bomey reports. 

 A global cattle shortage has driven up the cost for red meat, making burgers and steaks more expensive. 🤳 Trump said on Truth Social that he had "concluded a deal to substantially lower the price of ground beef for working American families."

 He added, "We have a commitment that this beef will be sold at 25 percent below current market prices." "As we work to rebuild this herd and help our ranchers, for the next 90 days, the United States will allow up to 300,000 metric tons of product for ground beef to be imported with no out of quota tariff." 🤔 Reality check: Trump didn't specify who made the commitment to sell the beef at a lower price.

 A White House official said the commitment came from foreign beef exporters, and that the 25% discount would be passed along to U.S. consumers. Go deeper . 

 
 
